<section style="padding: 6rem 0 3rem; background:#000;">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 1100px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.5rem;">
    <p style="font-size:0.65rem;letter-spacing:0.3em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#e00;margin-bottom:0.5rem;">Sourcing Decision Guide</p>
    <h1 style="font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3rem);font-weight:200;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.25rem;line-height:1.2;">
      Direct Factory vs. Trading Company vs. Buying Agent
    </h1>
    <p style="color:#ccc;max-width:760px;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.95rem;">
      Half the "clothing manufacturers" that email you back aren't manufacturers at all. Some are trading companies reselling access to a factory they don't own. Some are buying agents working on your behalf. A few are the real thing — a factory that actually cuts, sews, and ships your order. None of that is obvious from an inbox, so we wrote this to make it obvious. It includes where we fall short too, because a comparison that only flatters one side isn't worth reading.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#888;font-size:0.8rem;margin-top:1rem;">Written by <a href="/about" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">Chowdhury Remon</a>, Founder & CEO, SDF Clothing · Last reviewed {lastReviewed}</p>
  </div>
</section>

