<section style="padding: 3rem 0 5rem; background:#000; border-top:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.5rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size:1.6rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:2rem;">Pros and Cons of Each Sourcing Model (The Honest Version)</h2>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.15rem;color:#e00;margin-bottom:1rem;">SDF Clothing (Direct Factory)</h3>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><strong>What works in our favour:</strong> we own the whole process, yarn to finished garment, so there's nowhere for a defect to hide behind a subcontractor. Our <a href="/certifications" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">certifications</a> are audited under our own name, not borrowed. If a sample comes back wrong, we fix it and send it again — up to 10 rounds — before bulk ever starts. We also give buyers free tools to check our numbers instead of taking our word for it, and we'll let serious buyers visit or audit under NDA.</p>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:2rem;"><strong>Where we fall short:</strong> we're in Dhaka, so shipping takes longer than a factory down the road from you. And our 300-piece MOQ is higher than the "50 pieces" some overseas sellers advertise — though most of them can't actually produce past a few hundred, and we can.</p>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.15rem;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1rem;">Trading Company</h3>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><strong>What works:</strong> you get access to several factories without vetting each one yourself, and there's less upfront commitment.</p>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:2rem;"><strong>Where it falls short:</strong> you're paying a markup on top of the real factory price for a service that doesn't change the garment. Most of the time you can't confirm which factory actually made your order, and the certifications you're shown usually belong to that factory, not to the trading company.</p>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.15rem;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1rem;">Buying Agent</h3>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><strong>What works:</strong> the agent is on your side, not the factory's, and a decent one will arrange independent inspection — that's a real safeguard, not a sales line.</p>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:2rem;"><strong>Where it falls short:</strong> the agent doesn't own a factory, so your capacity and certifications depend on whichever one they pick that month. And there's an extra person between you and whoever is actually making your clothes.</p>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.15rem;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1rem;">Local / Boutique Factory</h3>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><strong>What works:</strong> same time zone, easier to visit, faster turnaround on small batches.</p>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;">
      <strong>Where it falls short:</strong> most cap out well before a few thousand pieces, usually cut-and-sew only so you're sourcing your own fabric, and formal certifications are hard to justify at that scale — the cost doesn't make sense yet.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding: 3rem 0 5rem; background:#000; border-top:1px solid #1a1a1a;">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1.5rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size:1.6rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Which One Fits You?</h2>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.92rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">There's no single right answer here — it depends on where you are. This is how we'd point most buyers, based on what we actually see from this side of the table:</p>
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:0.85rem;color:#ddd;margin-bottom:2rem;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
          <th style="text-align:left;padding:0.6rem;color:#fff;">If you're...</th>
          <th style="text-align:left;padding:0.6rem;color:#fff;">Usually a better fit</th>
          <th style="text-align:left;padding:0.6rem;color:#fff;">Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding:0.6rem;">A first-time brand, unsure of specs</td><td style="padding:0.6rem;">Buying agent</td><td style="padding:0.6rem;">Fewer costly mistakes while you learn the process</td></tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding:0.6rem;">Ordering a mix of small quantities across many styles</td><td style="padding:0.6rem;">Trading company</td><td style="padding:0.6rem;">Access to variety without vetting a factory for each style</td></tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding:0.6rem;">You know your specs and plan to reorder</td><td style="padding:0.6rem;">Direct factory</td><td style="padding:0.6rem;">No markup, no agent fee, faster answers</td></tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding:0.6rem;">Scaling past a few thousand pieces per style</td><td style="padding:0.6rem;">Direct factory</td><td style="padding:0.6rem;">Most trading companies and agents are limited by whichever factory they're subcontracting that month</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1rem;">A rough sense of where the cost difference comes from</h3>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">Say a factory's real price for a garment is $5.00 FOB. As a rough illustration of how each model typically prices the same garment:</p>
    <ul style="color:#ddd;line-height:2;font-size:0.9rem;padding-left:1.2rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
      <li><strong>Direct factory:</strong> ~$5.00 — the price you'd get talking to the factory directly</li>
      <li><strong>Trading company:</strong> ~$5.75–$6.50 — the same garment plus their markup for arranging it</li>
      <li><strong>Buying agent:</strong> ~$5.00 + a commission (commonly around 5–10%, agent-dependent) — closer to the factory price, since the agent isn't buying and reselling the goods themselves</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="color:#888;font-size:0.8rem;margin-bottom:2rem;">These are illustrative ranges to explain where the cost sits, not a quote — get an actual <a href="/tools/price-calculator" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">FOB estimate</a> or <a href="/tools/garment-cost-calculator" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">landed cost calculation</a> for your specific order.</p>

    <h3 style="font-size:1.1rem;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1rem;">A quick way to think it through</h3>
    <p style="color:#ddd;line-height:1.8;font-size:0.9rem;">Need a custom product at high volume? Go direct to a factory. Need it customized but you're still small or unsure? A buying agent is worth the commission while you learn. Need a bit of everything in small quantities, and don't want to manage multiple factory relationships yourself? A trading company earns its markup. None of this is permanent — plenty of buyers start with an agent and move direct once they know what they're doing.</p>
  </div>
</section>

